.TH SIGNAL 2
.SH NAME
signal, sigreturn \- what a signal does to this process (SYS_SIGNAL, SYS_SIGRETURN)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

sighandler_t signal(int sig, sighandler_t fn);

syscall4(SYS_SIGNAL, sig, entry, 0, 0);
syscall4(SYS_SIGRETURN, 0, 0, 0, 0);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B SYS_SIGNAL
sets what signal
.I sig
does to the calling process:
.B SIG_DFL
(the default: the process ends),
.B SIG_IGN
(nothing), or a function that the calling thread runs when the signal
comes:
.IR entry ( sig ,
.IR regs ),
on its own stack, where
.I regs
is a copy of the registers the signal found it with
.RB ( mk_regs_t ,
read only). The thread's registers stay in the kernel meanwhile; the
function starts with fresh floating-point registers. It ends with
.BR SYS_SIGRETURN ,
which takes the thread back where the signal found it, all its registers
as they were. If the thread waited (a message, a reply, a sleep, a
child), that call returns
.BR EINTR .
While the function runs, further signals wait (or are taken by
.BR sigwait (2)).
.B SIGKILL
cannot be caught or ignored.
.PP
The library's
.B signal
keeps one function per signal and gives the kernel its own entry, which
calls it then
.BR SYS_SIGRETURN .
The function must return, not jump out. It returns the previous
function, or
.B SIG_ERR
for a signal that cannot be caught.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR SYS_SIGNAL :
0, or a negative error.
.BR SYS_SIGRETURN
does not return to its caller; outside a signal's function it fails.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.RB ( SYS_SIGNAL )
.I sig
is out of range or
.BR SIGKILL ,
or
.I entry
is not a user address.
.RB ( SYS_SIGRETURN )
The thread is not in a signal's function.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kill (2),
.BR tkill (2),
.BR sigwait (2),
.BR kill (1)
